<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Tethba</h1><p class="st-meta">500 CE – 1200 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A midland Irish kingdom spanning Longford and Westmeath, held by branches of the southern Ui Neill and later by the O'Farrells and O'Quinns.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Tethba appears in the earliest Irish annals as a territory in the Irish midlands, divided by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-inny">River Inny</a> into northern and southern parts. Its rulers in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a> came from Cairpre and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-maine">Maine</a>, sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niall-of-the-nine-hostages">Niall of the Nine Hostages</a>, making it part of the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ui-neill">Ui Neill</a> world centred on Mide.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Tethba was a small overkingship of several local peoples, often subordinate to the kings of Mide. Monasteries such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardagh-county-longford">Ardagh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clonmacnoise">Clonmacnoise's</a> daughter houses stood in or near its lands. In the 11th and 12th centuries local families, the Ui Cuinn and the Ui Fergail, rose to power in the northern part, later known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annaly">Annaly</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the Norman invasion much of southern Tethba was granted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-de-lacy-lord-of-meath">Hugh de Lacy</a> within the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-meath">Lordship of Meath</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annaly">Annaly</a> survived under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ofarrell">O'Farrells</a> until the 16th century, when it became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-longford">County Longford</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 500 CE – 1200 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">British Isles</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:69.65%;width:13.93%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>500 CE – 1200 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old and Middle Irish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ireland">Ireland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tethba, Teffia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=850&amp;focus=kingdom-of-tethba" aria-label="Kingdom of Tethba on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4494 367 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Tethba lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4853 592h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4814 623h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M4814 623h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Ireland. <a href="/atlas?year=850&amp;focus=kingdom-of-tethba">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

